Revision history for OCP

{{$NEXT}}
    - A clone's `.ocp/` now gets the `.gitignore` `ocp init` writes, from
      whichever command makes the directory first (the first PIN1 unlock,
      status, known_hosts), so the decrypted age key stays out of git even
      where the project's own `.gitignore` does not cover it.
    - On a fresh clone, `ocp status`, `ocp kubeconfig`, `ocp node ls`,
      `ocp health`, `ocp provider ls`, `ocp deploy-robocop`, `ocp hetzner
      list` and the other commands that read `kubeconfig.yaml` or
      `secrets.yaml` first now ask for PIN1 and unlock the project key,
      instead of dying with "Error open (<) on '.ocp/age.key'". Without
      `age.key.enc` they say where the key comes from.
    - `ocp apply --dry-run` records no host key in `.ocp/known_hosts` any
      more: its host probes contact only machines whose key is recorded,
      checked against it, and skip the others with that reason; on a clone
      they read the robo key with the key PIN1 opened. A changed host key is
      reported on STDERR also when a probe used the robo key.
    - `ocp keys operator add|rm`, `ocp keys secure --drop-bootstrap` and
      `ocp node ls` no longer run out of memory on a worker pool whose
      `nodes:` is a list, or a huge number on a pool whose provider is
      unknown or missing: such a `nodes:` reads as one worker, and
      `validate()` refuses the pool anyway.
    - `ocp apply`'s closing banner reads WITH WARNINGS, not SUCCESSFULLY,
      when an OCPNode status could not be written, an old robocop pod could
      not be listed or deleted, or the control plane's Node or the robocop
      Deployment could not be read; the exit code stays 0.
    - An error from the Hetzner API no longer shows the API token, and no
      longer ends in "This is an internal error": `ocp apply`, `ocp
      destroy`, `ocp hetzner list`, `ocp node rm` and robocop (which writes
      it into an OCPNode's status) print it as one line, the token as
      `********` and without a Perl source location, as `ocp init` and `ocp
      keys secure` already did. `--verbose` prints the masked line as well.
    - `ocp apply` refuses a key under `kubernetes:` or `ssh:` that is none
      of their own, and one on an ssh pool's machine other than `host`,
      naming the keys taken: `kubernetes: { distribution: k3s }` was read
      by nothing and the cluster came up as RKE2 -- it now says to write
      `dist: k3s`. It also refuses a `kubernetes.version` or a control
      plane's `name` that is a list, a mapping or a boolean.
    - `ocp apply --dry-run` on a secure-mode project no longer writes the
      project key: PIN1 opens it in memory, so a dry run leaves no
      `.ocp/age.key` or `.ocp/age.pub` behind, and works on a fresh clone
      without `.ocp/`, against an existing cluster too.
    - `ocp keys secure` masks the Hetzner API token when storing the keys
      in the Hetzner project fails, as `ocp init` does: an error that
      carries the request no longer shows the token.
    - On a fresh clone of a secure-mode project (encrypted files committed,
      no `.ocp/`), the first PIN1 no longer ends in "Cannot create
      .ocp/age.key securely: No such file or directory": `ocp keys ...`,
      `ocp ssh`, `ocp inject-key` and a first `ocp apply` make `.ocp/`
      themselves.
    - `ocp apply` re-applies NFD or the GPU Operator when its Deployment
      was changed in the cluster to another image tag than its pin. `ocp
      status` already counted that as drift `ocp apply` fixes, but both
      steps compared only what they had recorded and called it up to date;
      the dry run now says it would re-apply the manifest, and status, the
      dry run and the real run agree.
    - `ocp node add` (without robocop) honours `gpu.enabled: false` and
      `gpu.driver: operator` from `ocp.yaml` on the node it joins, as
      robocop does: no GPU detection under the kill switch, `--gpu`
      included, and no host driver under the operator driver.
    - `ocp apply` and `ocp deploy-robocop` say on STDERR, with the cause,
      when the status of the control plane's or a migrated node's OCPNode
      could not be written, or when robocop pods from before SIGTERM
      handling could not be listed or deleted -- no longer on STDOUT among
      the progress lines.
    - `ocp keys secure` and `ocp keys operator add|rm` name why a machine
      failed without any Perl source location in it, as `ocp init` does:
      one in the middle of a wrapped error no longer stays in the line.
    - `ocp keys operator add|rm` and `ocp keys secure --drop-bootstrap` stop
      on a control plane or worker pool in `ocp.yaml` that is no mapping, a
      `control_planes` or `workers` of the wrong shape, a host that is a
      list, or a pool's `nodes` its provider cannot read -- with
      `validate()`'s line on STDERR and exit 1, before PIN1 is asked, the
      cluster is read or a machine is touched -- instead of an internal
      error after PIN1, or taking a `control_planes` string for no control
      plane.
    - `gpu.amd.integrated: true` in `ocp.yaml` reaches every node, not
      only one joined with `ocp node add --gpu`: `ocp apply` writes it
      onto the OCPNodeProvider CR as `spec.gpu.amd.integrated`, robocop,
      `ocp apply` and `ocp node add` hand it to the join, and the first
      control plane gets it from the bootstrap. The line for a skipped
      integrated AMD GPU told the user to set it while nothing read it. A
      worker (or a second control plane) that has already joined gets it
      only when it is joined again; a provider CR an older `ocp` wrote
      reads as false.
    - `ocp apply` no longer says every worker in `ocp.yaml` has an OCPNode
      when one of them matches an OCPNode only by its host.
    - `ocp apply` takes only a 404 for a Node, OCPNode or robocop
      Deployment that is not there in its CR steps; a 403 or 500 is named
      with its cause on STDERR or in the worker's result instead.
    - `ocp node add --gpu` checks the `gpu:` block of `ocp.yaml` before it
      asks the cluster anything or touches the machine, and refuses a
      malformed one -- such as `gpu.enabled: no` -- naming every line,
      instead of stopping on it after the node had joined.
    - `ocp apply` refuses a key on a worker pool or a control plane that
      nothing reads, naming the keys it takes: a pool's `count:` (read by
      nothing since February, the pool got one machine) now says to write
      `nodes:`, and a typo such as `lables:` no longer passes in silence.
    - An `ocp.yaml` that is empty or no mapping stops every command with
      one line naming the file instead of an internal error. `ocp apply`
      also refuses a `kubernetes.dist` other than `rke2` or `k3s` (`dist:
      rke3` installed RKE2 and went out to robocop as such), an `ssh:`
      that is no mapping or a key path that is no string, a control
      plane's `public_ip` or `private_ip` that is a list or mapping, and a
      `system.timezone` or `system.locale` that is a list or mapping.
    - `ocp destroy` with nothing recorded no longer lists a hetzner control
      plane from `ocp.yaml` as "Deleting <cluster>-cp-1..." and then
      deletes nothing: it says that server is found by its `ocp-cluster`
      label or not at all, and, when no Hetzner token could search for it
      or the search failed, says on STDERR that it may still run, with the
      `hcloud` selector to look for it by hand. The report of servers
      mislabelled before k98 now runs when no node is found as well. A
      control plane with `host: 0` is no machine named `0`, a hetzner
      control plane's `host` no longer holds a teardown up, and a worker
      pool with `provider: false` is skipped as "provider required", the
      words `ocp apply` refuses it with.
    - `ocp apply` redeploys an AMD GPU DaemonSet (device plugin, labeller,
      DRA driver, metrics exporter) whose image was changed in the cluster
      to another tag than its pin. `ocp status` already counted that as
      drift `ocp apply` fixes, but the AMD step only compared what it had
      recorded and left it, and the dry run called it up to date; status,
      the dry run and the real run now agree.
    - `ocp apply` takes robocop's credentials Secret for missing only when
      the cluster answers 404: a read it refuses or fails (403, 5xx, a
      broken connection) fails the robocop step with its cause on STDERR,
      before a PIN2 prompt, an SSH read of the join token or a write --
      instead of writing over a Secret it could not see, or telling an
      operator run that the Secret does not exist. `ocp apply --dry-run`
      says the Secret could not be checked, with the cause, instead of
      "missing, would write it". `ocp keys secure` stops its robocop part
      with the cause on such a read of the robocop Deployment, instead of
      leaving a running robocop on its old credentials.
    - `ocp apply --dry-run` on a fresh dev-mode project no longer writes
      `.ocp/age.key` and `.ocp/age.pub`: it says that a real run
      generates the age key, and leaves the project directory as it
      found it.
    - `ocp apply --dry-run` says what the AMD GPU step would do: per
      component (device plugin, DRA driver, labeller, metrics exporter),
      that a real run would deploy, redeploy, update or remove it, at
      which pin and on which nodes, or that it is up to date. It reads the
      Nodes, `.ocp/deployed.yaml` and the DaemonSets and changes none of
      them; a read it cannot make is "could not be checked", with the
      cause on STDERR.
    - `ocp apply` and `ocp status` read `registry.local` in the CoreDNS
      Corefile the same way, from the server block CoreDNS answers it
      from. A Corefile without a root block whose own `registry.local`
      block maps the name to the control plane is up to date instead of a
      "no server block for the root zone" warning. A block of its own that
      maps it elsewhere, which shadows the record `ocp apply` writes into
      the root block, is named by both -- on STDERR by `ocp apply`, and by
      `ocp status` as drift `ocp apply` does not close, with what to change
      -- and `ocp apply` no longer writes a record there that nothing reads.
    - A Rex task that dies before rex runs -- IPC::Run not finding rex,
      say -- no longer leaves its environment behind in the process that
      started it: the task parameters (a join token among them), the key
      paths, the known_hosts file and OCP's lib/ on PERL5LIB stayed set
      for every later child of that process, robocop's included.
    - `ocp status` counts a worker pool's machines the way `ocp apply`
      builds them: the hosts an ssh pool names (its `nodes` list, else one
      for a `host:`), and `nodes` (default 1) for any other pool. A `count:`
      on a pool, which `ocp apply` has not read for a long time, no longer
      changes the number `ocp status` shows.
    - `ocp keys secure` checks `ocp.yaml` as `ocp apply` does before it
      asks for a PIN, reads the age key or touches a machine: every line
      `validate()` has goes to STDERR at once with "Nothing was changed", and
      the command exits 1. A malformed `name`, pod CIDR or robocop block no
      longer gets past both PINs and the switch to stop half way after
      `keys.yaml` and `age.key.enc` are written. `--drop-bootstrap` on a
      project that is secure already is not held up by it.
    - `ocp`, `robocop` and the Rexfile compare a YAML or CR true/false the
      same on either side of `eq` and `ne`: the correction for
      Cpanel::JSON::XS's operand swap now lives in `OCP::BooleanEq`, which
      `ocp` and `robocop` load and the Rexfile loads through the library
      path `ocp` hands the rex child, so a boolean on the right of `eq`
      no longer matches every string Perl calls true.
    - `ocp deploy-robocop` leaves an OCPNode or OCPNodeProvider CRD that a
      newer `ocp` installed as it is, with `ocp apply`'s `[skip]` line,
      judged by the one check both commands share. An older or absent CRD
      is still written; one that cannot be read stops the command with the
      cause.
    - `ocp node add --gpu` waits for the GPU label as long as `ocp apply`
      waits for NFD on a fresh node, ten minutes, instead of five: the NFD
      worker pod of a node that has just joined gets no sandbox until
      Cilium's first BPF compile there is done, and the shorter wait gave
      up on such a node where `ocp apply` would have waited.
    - `ocp apply` refuses a key under `gpu:` or `system:` that OCP does
      not read -- `gpu: { enable: false }` was a mapping without
      `enabled`, and left the GPU stack on -- and a `system:` that is no
      mapping, which it read on and died on inside Perl. `ocp update` and
      `ocp node add` stop with the same line on an unknown key under
      `gpu:` or `gpu.amd:`. A `host` on a hetzner or local worker pool is
      refused too: only an ssh pool's is read, and beside any other
      provider it named no machine.
    - `ocp destroy` reads a worker pool's `host` only on an ssh pool, as
      `ocp apply` does: a hetzner pool's `host` is no longer listed as a
      machine to destroy, a local pool's no longer sends the uninstall to
      127.0.0.1, and `host: 0` names no machine. A pool, or a control
      plane in the last-resort fallback, that destroy skips for a provider
      it does not know is named on STDERR instead of skipped without a
      word.
    - `ocp` flushes STDOUT after every write, so a log that takes both
      channels (`ocp apply > log 2>&1`, CI, a container log) shows each
      STDERR line where it happened instead of ahead of the progress it
      belongs to.
    - `ocp init --hetzner` says why it could not read the Hetzner
      catalogue: what the API answered (`unable to authenticate` for a
      token Hetzner does not accept; the status when the answer carries no
      message) or the connection that failed, on the one STDERR line that
      keeps the defaults, never with the token. Init still goes on.
    - `ocp destroy` also stops, with `ocp apply`'s line and before it asks
      for a PIN or touches anything, on a `name` that is a list, a mapping
      or a boolean -- `name: [prod]` searched for servers under a label
      none carries, missed every one `.ocp/status.yaml` does not record
      and still said "Cluster destroyed." -- and on a worker pool's or a
      control plane's own `host` of that shape, which became a machine
      named `ARRAY(0x...)`. Nothing else `ocp apply` refuses stops a
      teardown.
    - `ocp status` keeps its drift report over a `nocert` that is neither
      true nor false: the cert-manager check reads "could not be checked"
      with the line `ocp apply` gives, said once on STDERR, every other
      check runs and the exit code is 0 (`--json`: one entry of kind
      `error`). A worker pool that names its one machine with `host:`
      counts 1, where the spec summary said 0.
    - `ocp apply` fails the robocop step with its cause when the robocop
      Deployment cannot be read (an RBAC denial, an apiserver error)
      instead of taking it for absent, and writes nothing; registry.local
      DNS warns about a CoreDNS Corefile with no root server block instead
      of calling it up to date; the nfd-worker wait says why it ran out --
      how many of its pods were ready, no status yet, or no DaemonSet.
    - `ocp apply`'s first deploy says on STDERR when the control plane is
      not Ready after its 600s wait, with the Ready condition's status,
      reason and message -- or that no Node was listed, or the read that
      failed -- and says an unreachable API server there too. It no longer
      runs RKE2's kubectl and `cilium status` over SSH as a fallback,
      which on a k3s cluster called a path that is not there. The run
      goes on as before.
    - `ocp apply` refuses an ssh worker pool with neither `host` nor
      `nodes`, which got no worker and said nothing, and one with both,
      whose `host` was never read; `nodes: []` says a pool has no machines
      yet, and a host of `0` names none. A control plane's `location` or
      `image` and a pool's `server_type`, `location` or `image` that is a
      list, a mapping or a boolean is refused too. The mapping form with
      `nodes: N` says each of its lines once.
    - `ocp apply` refuses `gpu.enabled`, `gpu.toolkit`, `gpu.amd.labeller`,
      `gpu.amd.metrics`, `gpu.amd.integrated`, `lbipam`, `nocert` and
      `system.ntp` when they are not true or false -- `gpu: { enabled: no }`
      is a string, and turned the GPU stack on -- and tells a string that
      spells a boolean the one it meant. `ocp update` and `ocp node add --gpu`
      stop with the same line instead of reading the string as on.
    - `ocp apply` no longer takes a read it could not make (an RBAC denial,
      an apiserver error) for a missing object: `--dry-run` says the robocop
      Deployment could not be checked instead of that it would roll it out,
      the registry step fails with the cause, and the NFD and node-Ready
      waits name their last failed read; registry.local DNS with neither
      CoreDNS ConfigMap present is a warning instead of "up to date".
    - `ocp apply` on an existing cluster no longer ends its component
      summary with "All up to date" after a failed cert-manager or Cilium
      Gateway step or a Failed worker; the robocop fallback warning goes to
      STDERR; LB-IPAM on a loopback control plane fails with the cause when
      the Node list cannot be read, instead of building a 127.0.0.1 pool; a
      Gateway update reads "spec changed".
    - `ocp destroy` stops on a worker pool `nodes:` list that `ocp apply`
      refuses -- an ssh pool's entry that is no host name, address or
      mapping with `host`, or a list on a pool that counts its machines --
      with the same line, before it asks for a PIN or touches the cluster
      or a server. It died inside Perl ("Not a HASH reference") or listed
      the names as machines to destroy.
    - `ocp destroy` also stops, with `ocp apply`'s line and before it asks
      for a PIN or touches anything, on a `control_planes` or `workers` it
      cannot read: a control-plane list entry that is no mapping
      (`control_planes: [police1]` deleted the servers and removed
      `.ocp/status.yaml`, then died inside Perl with exit 1 and no report of
      mislabelled servers), a `control_planes` that is neither a mapping nor
      a list, a `workers` that is no list, a worker pool that is no mapping.
    - `ocp apply` refuses a `robocop:` that is not true, false or a
      mapping -- `robocop: no`, `off` or `"false"` is a string, and turned
      robocop on -- and a key under it other than `enabled`,
      `security_level` and `image` (`robocop: { enable: false }` was on
      too); a string that spells a boolean is told the one it meant.
      `ocp keys secure`, which reads the switch only after the keys are
      switched, stops there with the same line instead of rolling robocop
      out. `ocp apply` also refuses more than 7 control planes in the list
      form, a `host` or `server_type` that is a list, a mapping or a
      boolean, and a worker pool `nodes:` its provider cannot read: an ssh
      pool lists its hosts, any other counts its machines (a list there
      ran out of memory). A boolean `node_ip` is called true or false,
      not "a reference".
    - ocp apply no longer takes a refused or failed read for absence in
      the GPU node list, the GPU and AMD checks after a worker join,
      deployment readiness waits, the registry.local CoreDNS lookup and
      the LB-IPAM pool wait; it reports the cause. A GPU Operator whose
      ClusterPolicy is missing while the operator runs is re-applied.
    - ocp apply on an existing cluster says whether it created, updated or
      left the Cilium Gateway alone, and counts a change in its summary.
    - ocp apply reports a failed LB-IPAM setup or node-CR reconcile on
      STDERR and ends APPLY INCOMPLETE with a non-zero exit; before, it
      printed a [WARN] on STDOUT and exited 0. The registry.local DNS, NFD
      and NFD-label warnings go to STDERR with their cause.
    - A `true` or `false` from ocp.yaml or from an OCPNode or
      OCPNodeProvider no longer equals every word it is checked against:
      with Cpanel::JSON::XS loaded, as in the image, `'hetzner' eq true`
      held, so `provider: true` counted as a provider type wherever ocp
      compared that way (k305 closed `ocp apply`'s checks; this closes
      the rest). A boolean now equals only true and 1, or false, 0 and
      the empty string, on either side of the comparison.
    - `ocp status` counts drift that `ocp apply` closes without a remedy
      task -- a self-healing entry (NFD, the GPU stack, the GPU Operator
      CRDs, OCP's own CRDs) or a missing one (registry.local in CoreDNS)
      -- into "N of M can be reconciled automatically", and `--json` sets
      its `fixable` to true: `fixable` now means "ocp apply closes it",
      not "carries a remedy". Both count the entries `ocp apply --dry-run`
      would act on; report-only drift stays out.
    - `ocp status` and `ocp apply` (dry run included) report an OCPNode or
      OCPNodeProvider CRD in the cluster that lacks fields this ocp declares
      (the API server drops them without an error, as it did `spec.nodeIP`)
      or declares fields it does not know; `ocp apply` updates the older one
      and leaves the newer one as it is instead of writing it back.
    - `ocp apply` dies naming the directory when the package has no
      share/robocop/crds, instead of installing no CRD and saying nothing.
    - `ocp apply` now waits for `nvidia.com/gpu` in the allocatable of an
      NVIDIA worker that joins during the run also when the GPU Operator
      check before the join already ran; a node that does not get there
      is the usual `[WARN]`.
    - `ocp node add --gpu` and `ocp apply` now wait for an AMD node that
      joined an up-to-date AMD GPU stack until `amd.com/gpu` shows in its
      allocatable (a ResourceSlice under DRA), with 10 minutes for a
      freshly joined node instead of 5; before, they said "up to date"
      and returned while the node had no GPU.
    - `ocp apply` reads the Cilium Gateway first and says what it did:
      "Creating Cilium Gateway..." only when there is none, "already
      deployed (up to date)" when the apply changed nothing. A Gateway
      already accepted at its current spec is not waited on again. A
      read failing with anything but a 404 fails the Gateway step at
      once, with its cause.
    - `ocp apply` refuses an unknown key under `network:`,
      `network.lb_pool` and `network.l2` (a typo like `pod_cdir` leaves
      the default in force), and a `network.lb_pool` whose `start` is
      after its `stop`.
    - `ocp apply` refuses a node address (a control plane's `host`,
      `public_ip`, `private_ip` or `node_ip`, a worker pool host) or
      `network.lb_pool` inside the service network 10.43.0.0/16, the
      RKE2/K3s default that OCP does not change.
    - `ocp apply` names a `control_planes` entry, a `workers` value or
      entry, or a `gpu:` that is not the shape it must be
      (`control_planes[2]: must be a mapping (one control plane), not
      'foo'`), and a `control_planes.nodes` that is not a whole number
      from 1 to 7, instead of dying with a Perl error. `gpu: false` is
      refused with the hint `gpu: { enabled: false }`; `ocp update` and
      `ocp node add` refuse it in the same words.
    - `ocp apply` refuses a `name:`, `robocop:` or worker pool `name:`
      that is a list or mapping (a name also when it is a boolean), a
      `robocop.enabled` that is not true or false, `control_planes: []`,
      and a `control_planes.nodes` above 7, the most etcd members etcd
      advises; a huge `nodes:` no longer runs out of memory.
      `provider: true` and `robocop.security_level: true` are refused
      instead of passing as a valid choice. Error lines show a list,
      mapping or boolean as `a list`, `a mapping`, `true` or `false`,
      never as a Perl value (`ARRAY(0x…)`), and call a worker pool
      without a name by its place (`workers[2]`).
    - `ocp update`, when the version manifest lists manual steps, no
      longer takes a piped `y` as confirmation: without a terminal it
      reads nothing, says on STDERR that a person at a terminal is needed,
      and exits 1 with nothing changed. The question is asked on STDERR,
      only `y` or `yes` continues, and Ctrl-D aborts without a warning.
    - `ocp destroy` that finds no nodes asks before it removes
      `.ocp/status.yaml`, `.ocp/deployed.yaml` and the encrypted
      `kubeconfig.yaml`, and names them; it needs a terminal or `--force`.
      Without either it removes nothing and exits 1. Scripts that tidy up
      after an out-of-band teardown need `--force`.
    - `ocp destroy` without a terminal no longer takes a piped `y` as
      confirmation: it reads nothing, says on STDERR that it needs a
      terminal or `--force`, and exits 1 with nothing deleted. Scripts
      that piped `y` need `--force`. The question is asked on STDERR, and
      answering no exits 1.
    - `ocp deploy-image --cluster NAME` (or `OCP_CLUSTER`) refuses a name
      other than the cluster in `ocp.yaml` before it sends anything, as
      documented; the target was always the project's kubeconfig. The
      error for a `--cluster` contradicting `OCP_CLUSTER` names the value.
    - `ocp node trust-host` without a terminal no longer takes a piped `y`
      as confirmation: it exits 1 and keeps the pins; use `--yes` in scripts.
    - `ocp apply` refuses a `network.pod_cidr` (or the default
      10.42.0.0/16) that contains a control plane's `node_ip`, the IPv4
      part of a dual-stack value included, as it already does for `host`
      and `public_ip`.
    - `ocp apply` and `ocp apply --dry-run` check `ocp.yaml` first: on
      errors they list every one on STDERR and exit 1 before asking for a
      PIN or touching a host or the cluster. `gpu.driver` is checked too.
      A Hetzner control plane without `server_type` or `location` is now
      refused instead of being built as cx32 in fsn1.
    - `ocp apply` and `ocp apply --dry-run` no longer take a GPU Operator
      read the cluster refuses (an RBAC denial, an apiserver 5xx) for "no
      GPU Operator", "no ClusterPolicy differences" or "no ClusterPolicy";
      only a 404 means that. The read is reported as "could not be checked"
      with its cause on STDERR, "No drift detected" is held back, the dry
      run counts it with the checks that could not be made, and a real run
      ends incomplete over it.
    - When `ocp apply` does not complete, its closing hint points at the
      "Apply did not complete" line, which names what is unresolved,
      instead of "[!!] lines" a run without them (an unreadable drift
      check, a worker still Installing) never printed. `--dry-run` says "a
      real run leaves it as it is" for one report-only entry.
    - `ocp apply` and `ocp node add --gpu` now wait up to five minutes after
      the GPU Operator step until every NVIDIA node shows `nvidia.com/gpu`
      in its allocatable resources, so "GPU stack ready" means a pod can get
      the GPU. A node that does not get there is warned about on STDERR
      with what it shows instead (no GPU, a capacity left standing, or a
      read that failed); the apply and the join still succeed.
    - `control_planes[].node_ip` in ocp.yaml and `ocp node add --node_ip`
      set the address a node registers as its InternalIP (the RKE2/K3s
      node-ip), for hosts with more than one network address. Invalid
      values are refused before anything is installed or ordered. A node
      that registers under another address is warned about once, with the
      fix. Without a node_ip nothing changes.
    - Setting a node_ip needs a Rex::Rancher with node_ip support; until
      the image ships one, a node with a node_ip is refused with a clear
      message before the host is touched.
    - `ocp node add --node_ip` reads the cluster's OCPNode CRD first and
      refuses, before writing anything or contacting the host, when it
      cannot store `spec.nodeIP`; the message says to run `ocp apply`,
      which updates the CRD. An unreadable CRD refuses too, with the
      cause. Once the node is Ready, an OCPNode stored without the
      requested `spec.nodeIP` gets a `[WARN]` on STDERR.
    - `ocp status` counts NVIDIA GPUs by `nvidia.com/gpu` allocatable, so a
      node whose device plugin is gone shows 0 with its stale capacity
      beside it. In `--json`, `nodes[].gpu` now means allocatable (it was
      capacity) and every node gains `gpu_capacity` (a number, or null).
    - `ocp apply` no longer treats a drift probe it could not read (RBAC
      denial, API 5xx, TLS) as report-only drift. It says the component
      "could not be checked", no longer points at `ocp update`, and ends
      the run incomplete with a non-zero exit instead of "All N
      component(s) up to date". `--dry-run` says the same and counts it
      apart from the differences it would act on.
    - `ocp status` and `ocp apply` no longer read a drift probe that fails
      with anything but a 404 (an RBAC denial, an apiserver 5xx) as "not
      deployed". The GPU Operator and the AMD GPU DaemonSets were reported
      as no drift, Cilium, cert-manager, NFD and the Gateway API CRDs as
      missing. The GPU Operator CRD, registry.local DNS and pod CIDR checks
      behave the same, and the host probes no longer take a failed OCPNode
      list for a cluster without workers. Now it is an error entry, "drift
      unknown", with the cause on STDERR.
    - `ocp apply` no longer takes a failed Kubernetes read for "not there"
      when it deploys: only a 404 is. An RBAC denial or an apiserver 5xx on
      the registry, NFD, GPU Operator, cert-manager or AMD GPU checks fails
      that component, with the cause on STDERR, instead of redeploying or
      removing it. The readiness waits (AMD GPU stack, ClusterPolicy state,
      Cilium Gateway) name the read they could not make instead of saying
      "not found".
    - `ocp status` no longer reads a failure inside drift detection as "no
      drift": it says why on STDERR (`WARNING: Cannot detect drift: ...`),
      shows drift as "could not be checked" and exits 1. `--json` then
      carries one drift entry of kind `error` (component `drift`) and the
      `error` field instead of an empty list. A drift check that could not
      be read is shown as `ocp apply` shows it, "could not be checked" with
      its cause, and is no longer counted as drift.
    - `ocp status` knows OCP's own OCPNode and OCPNodeProvider kinds. On a
      cluster without the OCPNode CRD the host probes no longer report
      "drift unknown" for the workers; no CRD now reads as no workers
      recorded.
    - Hetzner server, location and server type lookups now read every page, so
      `ocp destroy`'s orphan sweep, the create idempotency check, `ocp hetzner
      list` and the `ocp init` pickers no longer miss entries past the first
      page. The server type picker decides deprecation and availability per
      location instead of on the top-level `deprecated` flag, which Hetzner
      removes from its API on 2026-11-02, and takes an optional location
      filter.
    - `ocp init --hetzner` asks for the location first and then offers only
      the server types that can be ordered there. A type that is deprecated
      or unavailable in that location is no longer offered, and locations
      with nothing to order are left out. When the default server type can't
      be ordered in the chosen location, it is not preselected.
    - `ocp hetzner list` no longer dies on the first server: the TYPE column
      reads the server type name WWW::Hetzner returns.
    - File::SOPS 0.003+ writes the SOPS lastmodified timestamp quoted on every
      write, so OCP no longer re-quotes it itself. `ocp apply` re-encrypts
      keys.yaml, secrets.yaml and kubeconfig.yaml whose lastmodified is still
      the bare timestamp File::SOPS 0.002 wrote (images 0219cfa to 80f3b36),
      so `sops -d` reads them again and File::SOPS stops warning; values and
      recipients are unchanged, and nothing is rewritten under `--dry-run`.
    - Host-side AMD GPU support: the Rexfile detects AMD GPUs and installs the
      amdgpu driver through Rex::GPU::AMD under `gpu.driver: host`, and
      registers AMD nodes with `ocp.internal/amd-gpu.present` and gfx-target
      labels, under `operator` too. Integrated GPUs are gated by the opt-in
      above. A detection failure under `operator` is a warning, under `host`
      it still fails.
    - `ocp update` knows the AMD GPU stack pins (`amd_device_plugin`,
      `amd_dra_driver`, `amd_metrics_exporter`): like NFD and the NVIDIA
      stack it leaves them to `ocp apply` and skips them when `gpu.enabled`
      is false.
    - ocp.yaml gains `gpu.amd` (allocation, resource_naming, labeller,
      metrics, integrated). `ocp status` and `ocp apply` report version drift
      of the AMD GPU DaemonSets (device plugin, node labeller, DRA driver,
      metrics exporter), against the new pins `amd_device_plugin` 1.31.0.11,
      `amd_dra_driver` v1.0.1 and `amd_metrics_exporter` v1.5.3. Integrated
      AMD GPUs are only set up on nodes joined with `--gpu` or when
      `gpu.amd.integrated` is set.
    - `ocp apply` deploys the AMD GPU stack on nodes the host step marked as
      AMD GPU nodes: ROCm's device plugin or DRA driver by allocation
      (`gpu.amd.allocation`, overridable per node with the
      `ocp.internal/amd-gpu.allocation` label), AMD's node labeller, and with
      `gpu.amd.metrics` the device-metrics-exporter. A component no node
      selects any more is removed. `ocp node add --gpu` sets up the AMD stack
      or the GPU Operator depending on which GPU the node shows.
    - `ocp status` lists AMD GPU nodes in its GPU section: per node
      `amd.com/gpu` allocatable (capacity only beside it: the kubelet keeps
      it after a device plugin unregisters), the gfx target label(s) and the
      effective allocation with its source (node label
      `ocp.internal/amd-gpu.allocation` or `gpu.amd.allocation`); then the
      devices per node in the ResourceSlices of driver `gpu.amd.com` and
      ready/desired of the AMD DaemonSets, DRA marked experimental. A missing
      DaemonSet reads "not deployed"; a failed read says "unknown" and puts
      the API error on STDERR. Clusters without AMD nodes print the section
      unchanged.
    - `ocp status --json` carries an `amd_gpu` object on clusters with an AMD
      GPU node: per node allocatable, capacity, gfx targets and the effective
      allocation with its source; the devices per node in the gpu.amd.com
      ResourceSlices; and ready/desired/state of each AMD DaemonSet. The
      output for other clusters is unchanged.
    - `ocp status` now prints a kubeconfig that cannot be decrypted, or a
      cluster API that cannot be reached, on STDERR with its diagnosis. The
      report stays on STDOUT and the exit code stays 1.
    - Dependencies move to the releases of 2026-10-21, all from CPAN, so the
      image vendors nothing: cpanfile raises the floors to IO::K8s 1.111,
      Kubernetes::REST 1.110, Net::Async::Kubernetes 0.011, File::SOPS 0.005
      and Rex::GPU 0.005 (Rex::LibSSH 0.005, Rex::Rancher 0.004 and
      WWW::Hetzner 0.102 were already asked for), and cpanfile.snapshot pins
      exactly these eight; no other distribution moves.
    - robocop's `host key of NAME (HOST) not pinned: ...` warning (k242) goes
      through robocop's own log (timestamped) and only when it first appears
      or its reason changes, not on every resync; once the key is pinned
      after such a note, one `host key of NAME (HOST) pinned` line follows.
    - `ocp node add --host NAME` with robocop off checks the host before it
      writes the OCPNode (k238): a name that does not resolve on this machine,
      and without `--nowait` a host that does not answer SSH with the join's
      key, is refused on STDERR with exit 1 and leaves no `Failed` OCPNode
      behind. With robocop running the OCPNode stays the request.
    - `ocp keys operator add` and `operator rm` list every re-encrypted file
      in their `[ok] Re-encrypted with a new data key:` line, keys.yaml
      included (k241); the line and the rotation message of `operator rm`
      now come from one list.
    - robocop no longer fails silently to pin the host key of a Ready worker
      (k239): the node stays Ready, `status.message` reads `Node joined and
      Ready; host key not pinned: <reason>` (an unresolvable name with the
      cluster wording of k240), the reason is warned in robocop's log, and
      the next resync tries again and clears the message once pinned.
    - A stale `Node joined and Ready; host key not pinned: ...` note no
      longer outlives the pins (k239): when the host key was pinned
      elsewhere (the CLI's trust on first use, e.g. `ocp ssh --node`),
      robocop's next resync resets `status.message` to `Node joined and
      Ready`. Other messages and control planes are left alone.
    - robocop's "name does not resolve" `status.message` no longer says
      "this machine (DNS or /etc/hosts)": the lookup ran in its pod, so it
      says the lookup failed inside the cluster (CoreDNS) and points at the
      cluster DNS or the IP address (k240). The CLI wording is unchanged;
      `OCP::SSH->unresolved_message` takes an optional `context => 'cluster'`.
    - robocop's SSH wait on a host name that does not resolve now uses the
      cluster wording of k240 too: `status.message` reads `SSH not ready:
      Name '...' does not resolve: the lookup failed inside the cluster ...`
      instead of "this machine (DNS or /etc/hosts)" (k240). The CLI's
      message is unchanged.
    - `ocp ssh` explains a refused key with the same diagnosis as the SSH
      wait (which key, `ocp keys authorized-keys --purpose ...`) before
      handing over to ssh (k237). Host probes use the operator key
      (OCP_OPERATOR_KEY) under an operator identity instead of being
      skipped; a set but unusable key is warned about. `ocp keys
      recipients rm` and `ocp keys operator rm` name every re-encrypted
      file (keys.yaml included) in correct grammar.
    - A host name that does not resolve is reported as such instead of
      blaming the key ("the robo key was not accepted") or a generic SSH
      failure (k236): host probes, `OCP::SSH` and robocop's node
      `status.message` say so. `ocp node add --host NAME` warns, when
      robocop is on, that the name must resolve inside the cluster too.
    - Workers installed before the host-key pins (and ones whose pins were
      removed with `ocp node trust-host`) are pinned on the next reconcile of
      a Ready node (k235). `ocp ssh --node NAME` verifies the node's pins
      (refusing with the `trust-host` hint on a changed key; trust on first
      use for a node without pins). `ocp node rm` checks the host key before
      anything of the node is touched, so a changed key leaves phase,
      schedulability and workloads as they were.
      robocop's reconcile no longer pins the host keys of a control-plane
      node; the control plane stays on the project's known_hosts. A control
      plane pinned by the previous build is un-pinned with `ocp node
      trust-host NAME`.
    - `ocp apply` warns (k197) when robocop is on but no control plane is
      amd64 (every one a Hetzner `cax*` server): the robocop image is amd64
      only, so the pod would stay Pending and worker joins through robocop
      would not run. `[WARN]` up front and after the health check (banner WITH
      WARNINGS), and an unschedulable robocop pod is named as that reason.
      Not a refusal; `robocop: false` or an amd64 control plane clears it.
    - `ocp apply` (reconcile) says what it used to leave out: a Registry setup
      failure is `[!!]` on STDERR and exit 1; a worker whose OCPNode is not
      Ready is `[WARN] worker X is <phase>, not Ready yet` (banner WITH
      WARNINGS, exit 0) or, Failed, `[!!]` and exit 1; "No drift detected" is
      held back while the GPU ClusterPolicy differs. The legacy containerd
      template probe covers ssh/local workers too; in secure mode without
      PIN2 the host probes use the robo key and a `[WARN] host probes skipped
      on <hosts>: <reason>` names what they could not reach. Its closing line
      reads `Legacy containerd template removed on <host>[, <service>
      restarted]`. robocop pods from before SIGTERM handling (no
      `ocp.internal/robocop-sigterm` annotation) are deleted with a 30 s grace
      period after the rollout. `ocp deploy-image`'s override hint is `[WARN]`;
      `ocp node ls --json` has `reconciler`.
    - `make smoke` can cover the worker path: `OCP_SMOKE_WORKER_HOST=<host>`
      (ssh worker, free) or `OCP_SMOKE_HETZNER_WORKER=1` (paid) joins a worker
      through an `ocp.yaml` pool, waits for Ready, checks
      `status.kubernetesNodeName` against `kubectl get node`, schedules a pod
      onto it and checks that `ocp node rm` leaves no agent / no server
      behind. `xt/lease-409.t` proves the OCPNode lease against a real API
      server (409 on a stale write, one winner of a race). See
      docs/development.md.
    - OCPNode host keys are pinned: the first SSH contact writes the machine's
      host key fingerprints (all key types) to `status.hostKeys`, and every
      later contact -- CLI or any robocop pod -- verifies against them. A
      changed key leaves the phase alone, says "host key of X changed
      (expected ..., got ...)" in `status.message` and touches nothing.
      `ocp node trust-host NAME [--yes]` forgets the pins on purpose. The CRD
      gains `status.hostKeys` (`ocp apply` / `ocp update` roll it out).
    - `ocp keys operator add|rm`: a Leitstand runs `ocp apply` unattended with
      its own age identity (SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE, which OCP now honours for every
      decrypt -- no PIN1, nothing copied into .ocp/, the project key stays a
      recipient of every write) and its own SSH key (OCP_OPERATOR_KEY, no
      PIN2). `add` puts the public key onto every machine first, then records
      it in keys.yaml (no private half) and .sops.yaml; `rm` takes it off every
      machine and revokes it. `ocp keys authorized-keys` includes operator
      keys. Under such an identity apply leaves an existing robocop Secret
      alone with a [WARN], fails on a missing one, and refuses a first deploy.
      `ocp keys operator add --name ... --ssh-pubkey ... --age ...` no longer
      fails with its usage message on every call; commands that take a
      positional word also find it behind an option (`keys operator rm
      --pins-stdin NAME`, `keys recipients add|rm --pins-stdin AGE`,
      `provider rm --pins-stdin NAME`, `node add --role worker NAME`, which
      named the node `--role`).
    - `ocp keys secure [--drop-bootstrap]` moves a --nopassword project into
      secure mode: it sets PIN1/PIN2, stages the admin and robo keys in .ocp/,
      puts both public keys on every machine over the bootstrap key, tests the
      admin key there and only then writes keys.yaml and age.key.enc. A failed
      host leaves the project in dev mode; a re-run continues with the same
      keys. robocop's credentials are rolled out again; --drop-bootstrap takes
      the bootstrap key off the machines.
    - `ocp keys authorized-keys [--purpose all|admin|automation]` prints the
      authorized_keys lines a machine needs, and nothing else on STDOUT. In
      secure mode a worker on the ssh provider needs both keys: robocop joins
      and tears it down with the robo key, the CLI (`ocp node rm`, `ocp ssh`)
      uses the admin key. `ocp init` and `ocp node add` say so; a refused key
      names the missing one and this command instead of `ocp inject-key`.
    - `ocp apply` no longer resets robocop to `:latest`: the image is
      `robocop.image` from ocp.yaml, else `raudssus/ocp:<version of the running
      ocp>` (a development checkout falls back to `:latest` with a [WARN]).
      apply prints `robocop image <live> -> <spec>` when it replaces a different
      live image; `ocp deploy-image` says it is an ad-hoc override.
    - robocop leases as robocop@<pod name> (POD_NAME, Downward API), not as one
      shared `robocop`: a successor pod no longer takes its predecessor's lease
      for its own. A live lease of another robocop pod is taken over once that
      pod is gone (404, Succeeded, Failed); otherwise it waits for the TTL.
      robocop handles SIGTERM -- no new reconciles, running ones finish and
      release their lease -- and the Deployment sets terminationGracePeriodSeconds
      600. status.reconciler is no longer overwritten by a reconciler that
      stepped aside for another's lease.
      The OCPNode CRD's status.reconciler accepts robocop@<pod name> (and still
      cli/robocop); with the old enum every robocop status write failed with a
      422 and new workers stayed Pending.
      robocop's memory limit is now 1Gi (request 256Mi); at 256Mi the controller
      was OOMKilled during every worker join.
    - First release. OCP is a Perl CLI that builds and looks after RKE2 or K3s
      clusters -- on Hetzner Cloud servers it creates, on machines you already
      own over SSH, or on the local host -- from one spec kept in git.
    - `ocp apply` turns an empty directory into a cluster you can put a workload
      on, and reconciles an existing one, installing in dependency order:
      RKE2/K3s, a docker.io pull-through cache and a local registry, Node
      Feature Discovery, the NVIDIA GPU stack, cert-manager, Cilium (eBPF CNI
      with kube-proxy replaced, Gateway API, LB-IPAM), and the CRDs with
      robocop. It is idempotent and ends on a real cluster-health verdict. On
      an existing cluster it also brings up workers added to `ocp.yaml` that
      have no OCPNode yet, and leaves the ones that have one alone.
    - `ocp` drives the control plane from a laptop or CI; `robocop` runs inside
      the cluster and reconciles worker nodes through the OCPNode and
      OCPNodeProvider CRDs, so nodes come and go on their own. Each node is
      reconciled in its own process, a few at a time, and a node still on its
      way to Ready is looked at again every minute (`ROBOCOP_RESYNC_INTERVAL`,
      `ROBOCOP_MAX_RECONCILES`). It learns the cluster's distribution and pod
      CIDR from the Deployment that `ocp apply`, `ocp deploy-robocop` and
      `ocp deploy-image` write, and never guesses them. robocop logs a
      startup line with its version, security level, namespace, distribution
      and pod CIDR, and exits with the reason on STDERR -- CrashLoopBackOff,
      not silence -- when either is missing or its watch cannot reach the API.
      Each reconcile logs the node, its phase and the phase it reached; a
      node that fails logs the reason on STDERR, and its log line and
      `status.message` say how to retry it (`ocp node rm`, `ocp node add`).
      `ocp node add` and `ocp node rm` drive the same objects by hand.
    - Spec and status stay separate: `ocp.yaml` (committed, snake_case) is what
      you want, `.ocp/status.yaml` is what the cluster has. `ocp status` reports
      the drift between them and against the pinned versions, and `ocp update`
      closes the gap for what moves in place. Its plan gives every pinned
      component an outcome: upgraded, left to `ocp apply`, skipped with the
      reason, or -- for a new RKE2/K3s pin -- refused before anything changes.
    - Every component version is pinned once and printed by `ocp version`;
      pieces that must move together, like Cilium and its Gateway API CRDs, are
      pinned together.
    - The Gateway API CRDs come from one channel, standard, which carries
      everything the pinned Cilium needs. Installs and Cilium upgrades apply
      them before Cilium moves; a failed apply stops either one. Drift reads
      the bundle version and channel off the CRDs, so a Gateway API pin bump
      alone, a non-standard channel or missing CRDs are reported and `ocp
      apply` re-applies the bundle and restarts cilium-operator.
    - Secrets, keys and kubeconfig are age/SOPS-encrypted in pure Perl, with no
      external tools, and are committed to the project. Two PINs stand in front:
      PIN1 unlocks the age layer, PIN2 a per-key password layer. Secure mode
      keeps a PIN2 admin key for anything a human triggers and an unattended
      automation key for robocop; `ocp init --nopassword` is a keyless dev mode.
      The decrypted state in `.ocp/` stays out of git in every case: init
      writes the project `.gitignore` also with `--nogit` (a project inside
      another repository), and `.ocp/` carries its own `.gitignore`.
      `ocp keys recipients ls|add|rm` encrypts them for further age recipients
      (a second machine, given only its public key), kept in `.sops.yaml` as
      sops does; add and rm re-encrypt with a new data key, and rm names what
      the removed recipient could already read and should be rotated.
      With `--pins-stdin` every command answers its PIN prompts, in order, from
      lines on STDIN instead of the terminal, and dies naming a missing one.
    - `robocop.security_level` decides how robocop gets that key: from a
      Secret (`secret`, or `secret_approved` behind PIN2), or `inject`, where
      `ocp inject-key` hands it over a port-forward into the pod's memory and
      nothing stores it; after a pod restart robocop stays not Ready and marks
      waiting OCPNodes `SSHKeyAvailable=False` until the next injection.
      With robocop enabled, every `ocp apply` -- fresh or reconcile, with or
      without workers -- makes sure it runs: the credentials Secret before the
      Deployment, as `ocp deploy-robocop` does, a current one left alone.
    - Machine host keys are trusted on first contact and verified on every
      later one, over ssh and Rex alike, against `.ocp/known_hosts` (robocop:
      a file in its pod). A changed key stops the run and names the command
      that removes the stale entry.
    - The RKE2/K3s cluster token reaches a node only through its config.yaml,
      written 0600 root:root; it never appears on an installer command line or
      in the service environment file.
    - Workers join the control plane at its `public_ip` when `ocp.yaml` pins
      one, else at its host, on every apply path and for RKE2 and K3s alike.
      An agent that has not joined within ten minutes stops the install with
      its join URL and the last lines of its journal. A worker `ocp apply` or
      `ocp node add` brings up is checked against the control plane first: an
      RKE2/K3s release of a newer minor stops the join before the machine is
      touched.
    - On K3s, Cilium is the only network stack: the server's config.yaml turns
      off Flannel, the built-in network policy controller and kube-proxy, and
      Cilium reaches the API server where the workers join the control plane
      -- its `public_ip` when `ocp.yaml` pins one, else its host -- with its
      pod pool on K3s' cluster CIDR. A Cilium that does not become ready stops
      the apply with its status instead of passing for installed. While a K3s
      node joins, its OCPNode status names a K3s agent, no longer an RKE2 one.
    - RKE2 and K3s share one pod network, `network.pod_cidr` (default
      10.42.0.0/16): it becomes the server's cluster-cidr and Cilium's pod
      pool, where RKE2 used to run Cilium's own 10.0.0.0/8 across most host
      LANs. `ocp apply` refuses a pod CIDR that overlaps the service network,
      a node address or the LoadBalancer pool. A running cluster keeps its
      pool through Cilium upgrades; `ocp status` reports one that differs
      from `ocp.yaml` and what moving it would take, and never moves it.
    - The NVIDIA GPU stack -- driver, container toolkit, GPU Operator and its
      ClusterPolicy, RuntimeClass, device plugin, DCGM -- installs only where
      NFD reports a CUDA-capable card, and `gpu.enabled` / `gpu.driver` in
      `ocp.yaml` govern it cluster-wide: every worker follows `gpu.enabled`
      unless its OCPNode sets `spec.gpu` itself, and `gpu.enabled: false`
      switches it off on every node. The ClusterPolicy and NVIDIADriver
      CRDs are NVIDIA's own, from the pinned GPU Operator release, so the
      ClusterPolicy OCP writes is accepted as written. `ocp node add --gpu`
      deploys the GPU Operator once NFD has labelled the new node, by the
      same check `ocp apply` runs, so a GPU worker added after the last apply
      does not wait for the next one; when it cannot, it says to run
      `ocp apply`.
    - Node preparation, the RKE2/K3s server and agent installs, Cilium with its
      Gateway API CRDs and readiness wait, the uninstall of an ssh/local
      machine, the check for a previous cluster's Cilium state, GPU detection
      from sysfs, and the NVIDIA driver and container toolkit come from
      Rex::Rancher and Rex::GPU; on Ubuntu `ubuntu-drivers list --gpgpu` names
      the driver package. A Kepler-or-older NVIDIA card gets no driver and
      makes no GPU node; a container toolkit already on the machine (a DGX's)
      is kept. With `gpu.toolkit: false` the GPU Operator runs no toolkit, and
      every node with a GPU, control plane and workers alike, gets its CDI
      specs from Rex::GPU instead -- only those that do not already resolve,
      so the operator validator finds `management.nvidia.com/gpu=all` and
      the node its `nvidia.com/gpu`. RKE2 installs from its
      checksum-verified release tarball, and a pinned version is checked
      against the installed binary. An install never moves a machine's RKE2 or
      K3s version: a server or agent already there keeps the version it runs,
      with a warning when the pin differs, and only a new machine gets the
      pinned one. The machine running `ocp` reaches the
      cluster API, from the end of the server install on, and GitHub while
      Cilium installs; robocop reaches the API of a control plane it joins.
      `ocp apply` on an existing cluster leaves RKE2 alone, and its
      cluster-cidr drop-in stays in force. A bootstrap run against an RKE2
      control plane that is already running (for example after losing
      `kubeconfig.yaml`) moves cluster-cidr into config.yaml and restarts
      rke2-server, then once more on the next such run, because the old
      drop-in goes only after config.yaml carries the same value.
    - The LoadBalancer pool and L2 announcement are configurable through
      `network.lb_pool` and `network.l2`, so a multi-node cluster hands out
      external IPs from a real pool instead of a node's own address. Without
      `network.l2.interfaces` L2 announces on ethN and predictable en* names,
      VLANs included, but not on switchdev fabric ports such as
      `enp1s0f0np0`.
    - Worker pools in `ocp.yaml` (`labels:`, `taints:`) and `ocp node add
      --label key=value --taint key=value:Effect` put labels and taints on
      their nodes: labels at registration where the kubelet may set them, and
      all of both through the API once the node exists. Changing an OCPNode's
      `spec.labels` or `spec.taints` later reaches the node, and `ocp apply`
      pulls a pool's changed labels and taints onto its existing workers too;
      only labels and taints OCP set are ever removed.
    - `cilium.devices` and `cilium.helm_values` in `ocp.yaml` reach Cilium's
      Helm values at install and on every upgrade, e.g. to keep Cilium off a
      RoCE fabric NIC.
    - No code path shells out to kubectl: every call goes through
      Kubernetes::REST and IO::K8s, and kubectl ships in the image only for you
      to debug with. `ocp deploy-image` and `ocp deploy-robocop` roll new images
      into the cluster over the API, and `ocp kubeconfig -e` merges access into
      ~/.kube/config.
    - `ocp destroy` tears a cluster down and deletes the paid servers it created.
      It stops robocop, then removes every worker -- read from the cluster's
      OCPNodes while the API answers, and from ocp.yaml's host lists -- before
      the control planes. Each Hetzner server is deleted with the token of the
      provider that created it, read from the cluster when `secrets.yaml` has
      none; a server no token reaches stops the run before anything is
      deleted. An ssh/local machine gets whichever RKE2 or K3s uninstaller it
      carries, server or agent, and then loses the Cilium eBPF datapath the
      uninstallers leave behind, so it can be bootstrapped again without a
      reboot; state that survives fails the uninstall with a request to reboot,
      and a part it had no tool to check (no `tc`, no iptables) is named on
      STDERR, and again at the end with the machines to reboot before their
      next install. A machine it could not clean is named at the end with the reason on
      STDERR, and the run exits 1. An install onto a host that still carries a
      previous cluster's Cilium state stops at once and says to reboot it,
      instead of stalling every image pull until RKE2 times out.
    - `ocp node rm` drains the node through the Eviction API, honouring
      PodDisruptionBudgets within a five-minute bound, then deletes its server
      or uninstalls RKE2/K3s from an ssh/local machine, the way `ocp destroy`
      does. A node it cannot clean up stays as Failed with the reason, exit 1.
      Deleting a worker OCPNode through the API does the same: its
      `ocp.internal/teardown` finalizer holds it until robocop has cleaned the
      machine, retried every resync on failure. A lease keeps robocop and
      `ocp node rm` off the same node; control planes are never torn down.
    - Invalid input is refused at the boundary with a message that names what
      was given and lists what would have worked, and errors carry no Perl stack
      trace unless `--verbose` is set. Multi-word options take either
      spelling, `--pins-stdin` or `--pins_stdin`, in any position.
    - Ships as a Docker image on a Docker-first toolchain, with CI that builds
      the image and publishes it to Docker Hub and GHCR.
    - `ocp node ls --json`, `ocp status --json` and the new `ocp health
      [--json]` print canonical JSON (sorted keys, no timestamps) on STDOUT,
      diagnostics on STDERR. `ocp kubeconfig --readonly [--name NAME]
      [--revoke]` prints a kubeconfig for a read-only ServiceAccount (view plus
      nodes and the OCP CRDs), without SSH and without stored state.
